  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,2.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$",
